  "result": "Fixed: the command now prints `27.06`, and all 7 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py` converts a price string to whole cents. When a price had only one decimal digit, it put the padding zero on the wrong side, so `\"12.5\"` became 12.05 instead of 12.50. That made 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax = `26.09`. Prices with two decimals were unaffected, which is why the existing tests didn't catch it.\n\n**Fix:** a one-word change in `shop/money.py:9` so the zero goes after the digit, giving 2 \u00d7 12.50 = 25.00, plus 2.06 tax = `27.06`. I also added `test_to_cents_single_decimal` to `tests/test_pricing.py`, which checks `\"12.5\"`, `\"12.05\"` and `\"12\"`.\n\n`python3 -m shop` itself still doesn't run because the package has no `shop/__main__.py`, so I tested with the `main([...])` form you gave. I can add that file if you want the shorter command to work.",
